  
   
   First off, MAKE SURE you start Strategic Falcon by invoking    
   "STRAT", not "SF".
   
   (Several tips for troubleshooting and general use are included 
   in the READ.ME file in your Strategic Falcon directory. Please 
   glance through that file as well as reading this one!) 

     Warning!  Warning!  Warning!  Warning!  Warning!  Warning!  

   
   Using Strategic Falcon, IT IS EXTREMELY EASY TO ALTER A 
   MISSION OR CAMPAIGN IN SUCH A MANNER THAT IT BECOMES 
   IMPOSSIBLE TO ACCOMPLISH!  Strategic Falcon will allow 
   you to change many aspects of a Falcon 3.0  campaign in 
   ways not intended by Falcon 3.0's designers.  It gives 
   you incredible power, but it must not be misused.  
   Please read the documentation completely before diving 
   into StratFalc!  You're responsible for your own lost 
   campaigns otherwise!  

   (Oh, all right.  We both know you won't read all the 
   documentation right now.  Please at least read the following 
   warnings.)

                        PRIMARY WARNINGS!!!

I.   IF A MISSION HAS BEEN ALTERED WITH STRATEGIC FALCON, 
     THOU SHALT LOAD THE STRTSPRT.COM TSR BEFORE ENTERING 
     FALCON 3.0.  IT IS HIGHLY RECOMMENDED THAT YOU INVOKE 
           STRTSPRT.COM IN YOUR AUTOEXEC.BAT.  

   Otherwise, Falcon 3.0 will hang after the current mission 
   is completed.  Details of STRTSPRT.COM use are presented 
   at the beginning of Section II in the manual.


II.  THOU SHALT NOT ASSIGN AN ALLIED FLIGHT TO ACCOMPLISH 
                    THE PRIMARY MISSION.  

   The rule is:  the primary mission objectives must be 
   accomplished by you or one of your wingmen, although that 
   pilot does not have to be in an F-16.


III. THOU SHALT NOT VIEW AN ALTERED AIRCRAFT IN THE 
            FALCON 3.0 "LOAD ARMAMENT" SCREEN.  

   If you change a wingman's aircraft from an F-16 to 
   something else, and load it accordingly (you have him fly 
   a B-52 and you load it with 24 Mk-84s, for instance), 
   remember you must never bring up that aircraft in 
   Falcon 3.0's "Load Armament" screen!  If you do, Falcon 
   3.0 will reload it to something an F-16 can handle;  it 
   doesn't know how to display 24 Mk-84s on a Viper!


IV.  THOU SHALT NEITHER ADD NOR DELETE GROUND UNITS IN     
            MISSIONS THAT SUPPLY THEIR OWN.  

   (These are normally any type of CAS mission.)  You can 
   move (a little) the units that Falcon 3.0 provides, or 
   even change the units' type (perhaps eliminate those 
   pesky SA-7s), but you should not try to use the units, 
   for instance, to attack a site.  This is because some (or 
   all) the units are bound to be destroyed in such an 
   attack, and their destruction will cause Falcon 3.0 to 
   assume that you failed your mission.  Save "Armor 
   Assaults" for missions where you are assigned only air-
   to-air tasks, in which destruction of ground units is, 
   for the most part, irrelevant.


V.   THOU SHALT FORCE FALCON 3.0 TO CREATE A NEW *.MIS FILE 
                       FOR EACH NEW MISSION.

   To make Falcon 3.0 do this, you have to hit the "Takeoff" 
   button in the final mission planning screen at least once 
   for every mission.  Then you can either (a) exit to DOS from 
   Falcon 3.0 by either pulling down the menu and quitting that 
   way (hit <ESC> <F> <Q>) or by using the <ALT-X> key 
   combination, or (b) you can return to the mission planning 
   screens by aborting through the menu (<ESC> <F> <A>).


     Thanks!  And get ready to fly Falcon 3.0 like you never 
                          have before!



